## How `Context.res` merges `Set-Cookie`

- **Lazy `res` creation carries prepared headers**: `Context.res` getter returns `this.#res` or creates a `new Response(null, { headers: this.#preparedHeaders })`, so headers set before `c.res` is materialized (including appended cookies) become part of the response (`src/context.ts:403-407`, `519-532`).
- **`res` setter merges old response headers into the newly assigned response**: when both `this.#res` and the assigned `_res` exist, it clones `_res` (`createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)`) then copies headers from the old response into it (`src/context.ts:414-434`).
  - Skips `content-type` (`418-420`).
  - **`set-cookie` is merged by taking all cookies from the old response via `getSetCookie()`, deleting any existing `set-cookie` on the new response, then appending each old cookie** (`421-426`).
  - All other headers are copied with `headers.set(k, v)` (`428`), so old-response values overwrite same-named headers on the incoming response.
- **Response-building merge path (`#newResponse`) differs**: when constructing responses (`newResponse`/`body`/`json`/etc.), it seeds headers from current `c.res` or `#preparedHeaders`, merges headers from a `Response`/init arg (appending `set-cookie`, setting others), then applies explicit `headers` arg (`string` => `set`, array => delete+append each). This path can preserve/add cookies rather than replace them (`src/context.ts:609-657`).
- **`c.header()` behavior**: writes to `c.res.headers` if present else `#preparedHeaders`; `append: true` uses `Headers.append` (used for cookies by helper), and if finalized it recreates the response before mutating when body not used (`src/context.ts:519-532`; cookie helper emits `c.header('Set-Cookie', cookie, { append: true })` at `src/helper/cookie/index.ts:101`).

## Where the merge can lose cookies

- **Reassigning `c.res` drops `Set-Cookie` values that exist only on the newly assigned response**: because the setter replaces the new response’s `set-cookie` entries with the old response’s cookies (`delete('set-cookie')` + append old cookies), any cookies set on the incoming `_res` but not present on the previous `this.#res` are lost (`src/context.ts:421-426`).
- Evidence: `src/context.test.ts:430-444` (“Should set cookie headers when re-assigning Response to `c.res`”) assigns a response with two cookies, then reassigns a different response containing `foo3=bar3`; the final cookies equal only the original two (`expect(c.res.headers.getSetCookie().length).toBe(cookies.length)` and `.toEqual(cookies)`), showing the new response’s cookie is not retained.
- By contrast, the `#newResponse`/helper flows tested do retain cookies when merging context/original-response inputs (`src/context.test.ts:447-454`, `578-592`), so loss is specific to the `c.res = ...` replacement/merge semantics above.

## Tests covering this behavior

- `src/context.test.ts:430-444` — reassigning `c.res` keeps prior cookies (demonstrates new response `set-cookie` not preserved by setter merge).
- `src/context.test.ts:447-454` — previous/prepared cookies remain when building a JSON response (`setCookie` + pre-set header).
- `src/context.test.ts:456-465` — multiple `set-cookie` values preserved when `c.res` is already defined.
- `src/context.test.ts:578-592` — `c.body()` retains cookies from context and from an original `Response` argument.
- `src/context.test.ts:148-155` — `c.header(..., { append: true })` appends values (generic header behavior relevant to cookie accumulation).
- Related cookie/header usage in tests/helpers: `src/helper/cookie/index.test.ts` (multiple cases asserting `res.headers.get('Set-Cookie')`, e.g. `169`, `181`, `194`, `209`, etc.) and helper implementation `src/helper/cookie/index.ts:101`.

